uplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_068309ef_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The topologist's sine curve listed is an example of what phenomenon?","options":["Path-connected but not connected","Open and closed in every metric","A single open metric ball","Connected but not path-connected"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"030c2424225f00ec4fe9f6dd","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_068309ef_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the definition require the path to map into $\\boldsymbol{E}$, not just into $\\boldsymbol{X}$?","options":["Because the journey must stay inside the subset being tested.","Because paths into $\\boldsymbol{X}$ are never continuous.","Because $\\boldsymbol{E}$ must equal $\\boldsymbol{X}$ for every path-connected set.","Because the metric is defined only on $[0,1]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1b01b6af6c5b3be4b32ad373","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_068309ef_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A set has no pair of distinct points. What does the given definition suggest about path-connectedness?","options":["The set is never path-connected.","The condition is vacuously satisfied.","A nonconstant path must still be found.","The set must contain the whole interval $[0,1]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"11006c6c0ec3d0f8aaa859e6","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_068309ef_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $E$ be a connected set that is not path-connected. Which statement about whether connectedness guarantees paths between every pair of points is correct?","options":["It must still be path-connected because every connected set is path-connected.","Connectedness gives paths between all pairs, but they may leave $E$.","Connectedness gives paths between all pairs whenever $E$ is bounded.","Connectedness alone does not provide paths between all pairs of points."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"68abecabff2a572dd91ae4ae","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_068309ef_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If for every pair $\\boldsymbol{a},\\boldsymbol{b}\\in\\boldsymbol{E}$ there is a continuous $\\boldsymbol{p}\\colon[0,1]\\to\\boldsymbol{E}$ with the correct endpoints, what classification follows?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{E}$ is not connected.","$\\boldsymbol{E}$ is path-connected.","$\\boldsymbol{E}$ is necessarily finite.","$\\boldsymbol{E}$ has no metric."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8f5e475a6fd2f83907a9c007","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_7f7c39a2_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Where must the path lie for path-connectedness?","options":["Outside $\\boldsymbol{E}$.","Only on the boundary of $\\boldsymbol{X}$.","Only at the two endpoints.","Inside $\\boldsymbol{E}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"78809b88392d1c00cf2a4e22","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_7f7c39a2_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is guaranteed by path-connectedness for points $\\boldsymbol{a},\\boldsymbol{b}\\in\\boldsymbol{E}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{a}=\\boldsymbol{b}$ for all choices.","A finite subcover of $\\boldsymbol{E}$ only.","A path $\\boldsymbol{p}$ with $\\boldsymbol{p}(0)=\\boldsymbol{a}$ and $\\boldsymbol{p}(1)=\\boldsymbol{b}$.","A derivative equal to zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4baf8d01abf4cfee1a0e2d20","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_7f7c39a2_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Why is an interval such as $[0,1]$ path-connected in $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["It contains no points at which a path would have to be defined or evaluated.","Any two points can be joined by the straight-line path between them.","It is disconnected because the endpoints are different points.","Paths are not allowed in $\\mathbb{R}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"564942101fa8b7aecdbe71c7","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_path_connectedness_7f7c39a2_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What relationship between path-connectedness and connectedness is stated?","options":["Every path-connected subset is connected.","Every connected subset is path-connected.","Path-connectedness and connectedness are unrelated.","No path-connected set is connected."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ddde82ecf8badc43516ab80d","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}